<figure
  class="essay-diagram"
  aria-labelledby="governance-diagram-title"
  aria-describedby="governance-diagram-caption"
>
  <div class="diagram-heading">
    <h2 id="governance-diagram-title">A workload crosses more than one boundary</h2>
    <p>The machine can be available while the work is still unauthorized.</p>
  </div>
  <ol class="diagram-flow">
    <li>
      <span class="diagram-step">Request</span>
      <strong>A person asks for an outcome</strong>
      <span>What result is needed, and what context may travel?</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="diagram-step">Placement</span>
      <strong>A coordinator finds capacity</strong>
      <span>Which machine is capable, reachable, and appropriate?</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="diagram-step">Permission</span>
      <strong>The machine owner can still refuse</strong>
      <span>Does this work fit the owner’s declared policy?</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="diagram-step">Receipt</span>
      <strong>Both sides keep a useful record</strong>
      <span>What ran, under which rule, and who carried the risk?</span>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <figcaption id="governance-diagram-caption">
    Finding available capacity answers only the second question. A governed system has to answer all four.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
